---
// What people ask before they connect, and where their own policies go instead.
import Icon from "../Icon.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/site";
---

<div class="v-head center">
  <span class="label">Questions</span>
  <h2 class="h2" id="cm-fq-h">
    Before you connect
  </h2>
</div>
<div class="v-faq">
  <details>
    <summary>
      Does it cost anything?
      <Icon name="caret" size={16} />
    </summary>
    <p>No. The community version is free for anyone, within the limits above.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
      Do I need an account or a token?
      <Icon name="caret" size={16} />
    </summary>
    <p>No. Add the URL to your AI assistant, and ask.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
      Which AI assistants can use it?
      <Icon name="caret" size={16} />
    </summary>
    <p>
      Any AI assistant that connects to a remote MCP server over HTTP, such as Claude Code, Claude,
      ChatGPT, Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot in VS Code. Claude lists it in its{" "}
      <a href="https://claude.ai/directory/dpolens">connector directory</a>.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
      Can it read my organisation’s policies?
      <Icon name="caret" size={16} />
    </summary>
    <p>
      No. It holds public law only. Your own policies need your own instance: run DPOLens yourself,
      or join the waitlist for DPOLens Cloud, an instance hosted for your team.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
      Is it legal advice?
      <Icon name="caret" size={16} />
    </summary>
    <p>
      No. DPOLens finds the clause and quotes it. What it means for your case is for the people
      responsible for it.
    </p>
  </details>
</div>
<div class="v-band">
  <p>Your own policies need your own instance.</p>
  <div class="btns">
    <a class="btn line" href={SITE.repo}>
      <Icon name="github" size={17} />
      Self-host
    </a>
    <a class="btn main" href="/#cloud">
      Join the Cloud waitlist
    </a>
  </div>
</div>
